A digital port of a board game I designed about modern attention overload. Everyone
at the table is trying to squeeze as much dopamine as possible out of a day, and the
thing standing in your way is that you only have so much attention to spend.

## How it plays

Each player gets a role that says what they actually enjoy, so the same activity is
worth more to one person than another and nobody is optimizing the same board. On
your turn you pick from six face-up activity cards. Every activity carries a
duration and a dopamine value, and the interesting part is that those two numbers
rarely agree with each other. The cheap hit is right there and it is tempting, but
the thing you would actually be glad you did costs most of what you have.

That tension is the whole game. It is the same argument you have with yourself when
you open your phone to check one thing.

## The port

The physical version came first, and the digital one exists because playtesting a
board game by hand is slow. In Godot 4 I can change a card's numbers and have an
answer in a minute instead of reprinting a deck.

It is a full game loop at this point. There are computer opponents to play against,
attention bars that respond as you spend down your turn, score tracking, a deck that
reshuffles when it empties, effect cards on top of the plain activities, and the
usual furniture of music, sound effects, and an options menu. It is all GDScript
against Godot 4.5, and it is still a work in progress, mostly on the balance side
where the card values are concerned.
